  <!-- Deep Scientific & Engineering Foundations -->
  <section class="bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-3xl p-6 sm:p-10 mb-8 shadow-xs">
    <div class="border-b border-hairline pb-4 mb-6">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mb-1">{d.scienceTitle}</h2>
      <p class="text-xs text-muted leading-relaxed">{d.scienceSub}</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 text-body text-sm leading-relaxed">
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mb-2">1. Thermodynamics of Water Dielectric Constant Tuning</h3>
        <p>
          At ambient conditions (25°C, 1 bar), liquid water is an intensely polar solvent characterized by a high relative dielectric constant (&epsilon; &asymp; 78.5) and extensive intermolecular hydrogen bonding. Under these conditions, water cannot dissolve lipophilic or semi-polar secondary plant metabolites such as flavonoids (quercetin, hesperidin), stilbenes (resveratrol), and proanthocyanidins.
        </p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs bg-surface p-2.5 rounded-lg border border-hairline my-2">
          &epsilon;(T) = 5321/T + 233.76 - 0.9297&times;T + 0.001417&times;T&sup2; - 8.292&times;10⁻⁷&times;T&sup3;
        </p>
        <p>
          As pressurized water is heated into the subcritical state (120–220°C) under sufficient hydraulic pressure (30–80 bar) to maintain the liquid phase, thermal expansion weakens the hydrogen-bond network. Its dielectric constant drops drastically into the range of &epsilon; &asymp; 30–35—closely mimicking the polarity of ethanol (&epsilon; &asymp; 24.3) and methanol (&epsilon; &asymp; 32.7). Consequently, subcritical water cleanly dissolves semi-polar and non-polar phytochemicals with zero organic solvent residue (0.00 ppm VOCs).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mb-2">2. Enhanced Self-Ionization &amp; Auto-Catalytic Deglycosylation</h3>
        <p>
          As water temperature rises from 25°C to 200°C, the ionization constant of water (K_w) increases by more than three orders of magnitude (from 10⁻¹⁴ up to approximately 10⁻¹¹):
        </p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs bg-surface p-2.5 rounded-lg border border-hairline my-2">
          2 H₂O &harr; H₃O⁺ + OH⁻  (pK_w drops from 14.00 to ~11.20 at 190°C)
        </p>
        <p>
          The abundant hydronium (H₃O⁺) and hydroxide (OH⁻) ions act as natural in-situ acid-base catalysts. In botanical matrices like red onion skin or citrus peel, this auto-catalytic hydrothermal activity selectively cleaves &beta;-glycosidic bonds—transforming bulky, poorly bioavailable flavonoid glycosides (rutin, quercetin-4'-glucoside) into high-potency, hydrophobic aglycones (quercetin aglycone) in under 4 minutes without synthetic acid catalysts.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mb-2">3. Thermal Degradation Kinetics &amp; Instantaneous Cryo-Quenching</h3>
        <p>
          While elevated temperatures accelerate solid-liquid mass transfer and solubility, thermolabile bioactive molecules (such as cyanidin and delphinidin anthocyanins) undergo accelerated thermal degradation following first-order Arrhenius kinetics:
        </p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs bg-surface p-2.5 rounded-lg border border-hairline my-2">
          k_deg(T) = A_deg &times; exp(-E_a / (R &times; T)), &emsp; t₁/₂ = ln(2) / k_deg
        </p>
        <p>
          To prevent pyrolytic ring cleavage and color loss, the continuous SCW skid enforces a short dynamic residence time (2.0–5.0 min) followed immediately by an in-line sanitary chilled glycol quench heat exchanger. The extract temperature is driven from ~150°C down to &lt;25°C in under 2.2 seconds, halting thermal degradation and preserving &gt;96% of active antioxidant capacity (ORAC, DPPH).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mb-2">4. Pressure Vessel Safety &amp; 3-A Sanitary Engineering Compliance</h3>
        <p>
          Operating subcritical liquid systems requires strict adherence to ASME and hygienic food manufacturing codes:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5 mt-2">
          <li><strong>ASME Boiler &amp; Pressure Vessel Code (Section VIII Div 1):</strong> High-pressure extraction columns, ceramic membrane housings, and heat exchangers are constructed from forged AISI 316L or Hastelloy C-276 with safety factors exceeding 1.50x relative to maximum allowable working pressure (MAWP).</li>
          <li><strong>3-A Sanitary Standard 04-05:</strong> High-pressure triplex plunger feed pumps feature solid ceramic plungers, food-grade perfluoroelastomer packings, and positive drainability.</li>
          <li><strong>3-A Sanitary Standard 44-03:</strong> Process conduits feature electropolished internal surface finishes (Ra &le; 0.38 &micro;m / 15 &micro;-in) with zero dead legs (L/D &lt; 1.5).</li>
          <li><strong>EHEDG Class I &amp; FDA 21 CFR &sect; 117:</strong> Automated Clean-In-Place (CIP, 85°C caustic/acid wash) and Steam-In-Place (SIP, 130°C for 30 minutes) protocols ensure aseptic integrity.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

